    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d419">

                <pb facs="0817" n="789" xml:id="pg789"/>

                <head>419. Telegram From the Embassy in Israel to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d419-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic">Tel
                                Aviv</hi></placeName>,<date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1955-11-19T17:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 19,
                                1955—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>527.Herzog, Chief American Section Foreign Ministry, yesterday referred to Ben
                    Gurion’s November 15 conversation with Ambassador (Embtel 515<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d419-f2"/>) and inquired whether Embassy had received information
                    confirming or denying Macmillan’s message that Secretary was in full agreement
                    Eden’s statement. In replying I avoided any direct answer but made the following
                    points: <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>US area policy had been set forth publicly in Secretary’s August 26
                            address and President’s statement November 9.</item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item>The Secretary in his talk with Sharett at Paris<anchor n="3"
                                xml:id="d419-f3"/> had emphasized need for Israel to give urgent
                            consideration to sacrifices it would be willing to make to obtain
                            settlement.</item>
                        <label>(3)</label>
                        <item>The USG endorses Eden’s speech insofar as it supports the Secretary’s
                            August 26 address.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>In reply,Herzog in a tone of unwonted bitterness said that Nasser appeared to be
                    getting everything he wanted. He was getting his arms from the Soviet Union, the
                    High Aswan Dam from the US and now Eden had come along and offered him slices of
                    Israel’s territory. I commented it was not clear at this moment as to how any of
                    these 3 matters were developing but suggested that perhaps Israel was too prone
                    to look to the US to solve all of its problems and that the time had arrived for
                    GOI to do some hard practical thinking re bringing about an Israel-Arab
                    settlement. It appeared to me that if there was going to be a settlement an
                    accommodation by both sides was required.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">White</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
